## 1. Objective and scope

Determine why nominally identical 3D-printed polymer tensile coupons show persistent strength differences by:

- Quantifying the local sensitivity of tensile strength to small, controlled changes in manufacturing and testing factors.
- Separating true coupon-to-coupon variation from machine, batch, operator, environmental, measurement, and testing-system effects.
- Identifying which measured factors account for the observed differences and estimating their effect sizes.
- Distinguishing competing mechanisms, including:
  - interlayer or inter-road bonding differences;
  - voids, under-extrusion, and dimensional variation;
  - polymer moisture, degradation, or lot variation;
  - thermal-history and cooling differences;
  - raster orientation or toolpath effects;
  - spatial or temporal printer drift;
  - specimen conditioning and aging;
  - gripping, alignment, strain-rate, or metrology artifacts.

The primary response is ultimate tensile strength calculated from measured initial cross-sectional area. Secondary responses are elastic modulus, yield or proof stress, strain at break, failure location, fracture orientation, density, and process-stability measures.

The primary material, printer model, slicing profile, coupon geometry, and test standard should be fixed in advance. Any conclusion applies initially only to the local operating region studied.

## 2. Assumptions

- “Nominally identical” means coupons share the same digital geometry and nominal settings, not necessarily the same realized geometry, thermal history, internal structure, or test conditions.
- Current strength differences are reproducible and exceed the combined uncertainty of force and dimensional measurement; this assumption will be checked before causal experimentation.
- The manufacturing process is stable enough to estimate local effects, or drift can be measured and modeled.
- Factors can be varied around a defined baseline without changing the failure mode so drastically that local comparisons become meaningless.
- Local sensitivities are approximately linear over initially selected perturbation ranges. Curvature and interactions will be tested.
- Regrind or recycled content, additives, pigmentation, material lot, storage history, and drying state are controlled unless explicitly studied.
- Coupons are independent conditional on shared print-job and test-batch effects; coupons from the same build are not treated as statistically independent replicates.
- Testing follows one prespecified tensile standard, with consistent coupon type, conditioning, strain measurement, and validity criteria.
- Destructive testing makes repeated measurement of the same coupon impossible; paired comparisons will therefore use matched positions, mirrored layouts, or adjacent coupons.

## 3. Research design

### Phase A: Baseline reproducibility and variance decomposition

Print a balanced baseline series across:

- multiple independent print jobs;
- at least two material lots where feasible;
- multiple days;
- build-plate positions;
- coupon orientations and print sequences;
- test days and operators, if more than one is used.

Randomize coupon identifiers, testing order, and—where operationally possible—the assignment of jobs to printer, material lot, and day. Rotate or mirror build layouts between jobs so plate position is not confounded with print order or orientation. Include repeated builds at the beginning and end of the study to detect temporal drift.

Use a hierarchical structure with multiple coupons per job and multiple independent jobs per condition. Determine final replication using a prospective power or precision calculation based on pilot variance components, targeting effects small enough to explain a scientifically meaningful fraction of the persistent difference.

Before causal interpretation:

1. Conduct gauge repeatability and reproducibility studies for force, dimensions, mass, strain, temperature, and humidity.
2. Test whether strength differences are concentrated between builds, within builds, between plate positions, between lots, between test sessions, or in residual coupon-level variation.
3. Verify that failures occur in the valid gauge region and are not dominated by grip damage or obvious test invalidity.

### Phase B: Differential screening around the baseline

Define a baseline operating point and impose small positive and negative perturbations to plausible causes. For each continuous factor \(x_j\), estimate the local derivative using a balanced central difference:

\[
S_j \approx \frac{\bar{Y}(x_j+\Delta_j)-\bar{Y}(x_j-\Delta_j)}{2\Delta_j},
\]

where \(Y\) is tensile strength and \(\Delta_j\) is small enough to remain locally relevant but large enough to exceed factor-setting uncertainty.

Candidate controlled factors include:

- nozzle temperature;
- bed or chamber temperature;
- cooling-fan command;
- extrusion multiplier or commanded material flow;
- printing speed and acceleration;
- layer height;
- raster angle and coupon orientation;
- drying duration or moisture level;
- time from printing to testing;
- conditioning temperature and relative humidity;
- test strain or crosshead rate;
- grip pressure;
- alignment offset.

Record rather than deliberately perturb additional factors where precise control is impractical:

- actual nozzle, bed, chamber, and near-part temperatures;
- filament diameter and ovality;
- filament mass consumed;
- motor or extrusion anomalies;
- ambient temperature and humidity;
- power interruptions;
- print duration and layer timing;
- plate position;
- realized coupon dimensions, mass, and density;
- internal void fraction or bead geometry from nondestructive or sacrificial-section measurements.

Use a randomized blocked design, with print job as the principal block. Place baseline, \(+\Delta\), and \(-\Delta\) conditions within comparable jobs when feasible. If changing a factor requires separate jobs, balance sequence and include contemporaneous baseline jobs to estimate drift.

Initially use a fractional factorial or definitive screening design suitable for identifying active factors without changing many conditions at once. Preserve selected interaction estimates based on mechanism, particularly:

- nozzle temperature × speed;
- temperature × cooling;
- layer height × flow;
- moisture × temperature;
- raster orientation × force direction;
- conditioning humidity × time;
- geometry or void fraction × test rate.

### Phase C: Focused local response study

For factors showing meaningful effects, perform a higher-resolution local design using at least three levels around baseline. Include center-point replication to detect curvature and repeat the design in independent jobs.

For each leading mechanism, include a targeted intervention:

- **Bonding hypothesis:** vary thermal conditions while holding mass and dimensions as constant as possible; assess inter-road or interlayer fracture.
- **Void/under-extrusion hypothesis:** vary flow locally; measure density, void fraction, and effective load-bearing area.
- **Moisture/degradation hypothesis:** use controlled dry and equilibrated material, with verified exposure histories.
- **Spatial thermal-gradient hypothesis:** rotate coupon positions and directly measure local thermal conditions.
- **Testing-artifact hypothesis:** test a subset under deliberately varied alignment, grip pressure, and rate; where possible, retest test-system reproducibility using a stable reference material.
- **Batch or machine hypothesis:** reproduce the same local perturbations across material lots and, if available, printers to test generality.

### Phase D: Confirmatory prediction

Lock the model and factor set before confirmation. Produce new blinded coupons from independent print jobs, including:

- baseline conditions;
- conditions predicted to increase strength;
- conditions predicted to decrease strength;
- matched conditions designed to separate the two strongest competing explanations.

Predict both mean strength and uncertainty before testing. Confirmation must concern out-of-sample prediction, not only refitting explanatory associations.

## 4. Data or evidence needed

For every coupon:

- unique coupon, build, printer, material-lot, spool, operator, and test-session identifiers;
- complete slicer and machine settings, software and firmware versions, and toolpath hash;
- plate location, coupon orientation, print order, and neighboring-part layout;
- material provenance, color/additives, storage, drying, exposure time, and moisture measurement;
- timestamps for manufacture, conditioning, and testing;
- ambient, chamber, bed, nozzle, and near-part thermal records;
- actual or proxy extrusion and motion data, including anomalies;
- coupon width and thickness at multiple gauge locations;
- mass, apparent density, and, where available, void fraction and bead dimensions;
- surface and fracture images under standardized imaging conditions;
- raw force, displacement, extensometer strain, and crosshead data;
- calculated strength, modulus, yield behavior, elongation, toughness, failure location, and validity flags;
- calibration records and measurement uncertainty for all instruments;
- deviations, interruptions, visible defects, and exclusion reasons documented before inspecting group outcomes.

Evidence distinguishing explanations should include:

| Explanation | Supporting observations | Observations arguing against it |
|---|---|---|
| Interlayer/inter-road bonding | Strength responds to local thermal or cooling changes; fracture follows interfaces; strength changes without proportional mass or area changes | No thermal sensitivity; fracture is consistently through roads; interface metrics do not predict strength |
| Voids or under-extrusion | Strength tracks density, mass per gauge length, void fraction, or effective area; flow perturbations have the predicted sign | Strength persists after accounting for voids and geometry; thermal factors dominate at fixed density |
| Moisture or degradation | Measured moisture/exposure predicts strength; drying or controlled equilibration changes strength reproducibly | No exposure-response relation; dry and conditioned material have equivalent results within precision |
| Material-lot variation | Differences cluster by lot and remain under common settings and conditioning | Lot effect disappears after balancing process conditions or is not reproduced with new samples |
| Plate position or cooling gradient | Position effects rotate with layout or follow measured local temperatures | Coupon identity retains the effect after relocation; no positional or thermal gradient is detected |
| Printer drift or wear | Results trend with time, nozzle use, calibration, or maintenance and reset after intervention | No temporal structure; effects reproduce across independently maintained systems |
| Test artifact | Results track alignment, grip condition, rate, operator, or test session; failures occur near grips | Valid gauge failures persist under alternate fixtures, operators, or sessions |
| Geometry/metrology artifact | Nominal-stress differences vanish using measured dimensions or validated area measurements | Differences remain using independently measured effective area |
| Orientation/toolpath | Differences rotate or reverse with raster orientation and fracture direction | Similar effects persist under balanced and rotated toolpaths |

## 5. Measurement

### Tensile testing

- Use a calibrated load frame and a calibrated extensometer or full-field strain method over the gauge section.
- Apply a documented preload and alignment procedure.
- Record force and strain at sufficient sampling frequency to resolve yielding and fracture.
- Fix the strain rate according to the selected standard unless rate is an experimental factor.
- Photograph or video each test to determine fracture initiation and location.
- Prespecify invalid-test criteria, including grip failure, slipping, fracture outside the allowed region, signal loss, or protocol deviation.

### Geometry and mass

- Measure width and thickness at multiple gauge locations using a validated protocol that does not compress the polymer.
- Estimate both the standard nominal stress and a sensitivity analysis using minimum, mean, and spatially resolved area.
- Measure mass with a calibrated balance and calculate mass per unit gauge length and apparent density.
- For a stratified subset, measure internal void fraction and road/layer geometry using a validated imaging or sectioning method. Measurement analysts should be blinded to tensile outcomes.

### Process and environment

- Verify actual temperatures independently where possible rather than relying solely on commanded values.
- Log ambient and conditioning temperature and relative humidity continuously.
- Measure filament moisture with a repeatable method and include reference checks.
- Record filament diameter at multiple points and quantify ovality.
- Archive machine logs, slicer output, and the exact digital build files.

### Measurement-system assessment

Estimate:

- repeatability;
- between-operator reproducibility;
- calibration bias;
- temporal drift;
- resolution relative to expected effects;
- propagated uncertainty in tensile strength due to force and area measurements.

Where feasible, duplicate geometry measurements by blinded operators and process selected raw tensile records independently to quantify analysis-pipeline variability.

## 6. Analysis

### Primary model

Fit a hierarchical mixed-effects model for coupon \(i\), build \(b\), test session \(s\), material lot \(l\), and printer \(p\):

\[
Y_{ibslp} = \beta_0 + \sum_j \beta_j z_{j}
+ \sum_{j<k}\beta_{jk}z_jz_k
+ \sum_j \gamma_j z_j^2
+ u_b + v_s + w_l + q_p + \epsilon_i,
\]

where \(z_j\) are centered, scaled local perturbations or measured covariates. Treat factors as fixed or random according to the intended inference, and include nonlinear terms only where justified by the design.

Report:

- local derivatives in physical units and standardized units;
- confidence or credible intervals;
- variance components and intraclass correlations;
- partial \(R^2\), explained variance, and predictive improvement;
- residual variation after accounting for factors;
- interactions and curvature;
- out-of-sample prediction error and interval coverage.

Do not select causes using \(p\)-values alone. Require effect magnitude, uncertainty, repeatability across independent jobs, mechanistic consistency, and predictive value.

### Differential contrasts

For each deliberately perturbed factor:

- calculate within-block central-difference effects;
- check symmetry between \(+\Delta\) and \(-\Delta\);
- compare estimates across builds, lots, and plate positions;
- determine whether the perturbation changed suspected mediators such as density, void fraction, or thermal exposure.

If local effects differ by location or baseline state, report conditional sensitivities rather than a single global coefficient.

### Variance decomposition

Partition total variation into:

- material lot/spool;
- printer;
- job/day;
- plate position;
- test session/operator;
- measured coupon attributes;
- residual coupon-level variation;
- measurement-system uncertainty.

Compare the baseline model with models that sequentially add geometry, material state, process variables, structural measurements, and test variables. This identifies which factor groups reduce unexplained differences.

### Competing explanations and mediation

Use prespecified causal diagrams to distinguish confounders, mediators, and outcomes. For example:

- nozzle temperature → interlayer bonding → strength;
- flow → density/voids/effective load-bearing area → strength;
- humidity → moisture → degradation or plasticization → strength.

Estimate total effects from randomized perturbations and, separately, assess whether measured mediators account for those effects. Do not control for mediators when estimating total causal effects. Mediation claims require correct temporal ordering, intervention effects, and sensitivity analysis for mediator–outcome confounding.

### Robustness checks

- Analyze valid tests only and then repeat with all tests under transparent alternative rules.
- Compare nominal-area and measured-area strength.
- Use robust residual models if variance is heterogeneous or outliers remain.
- Check temporal autocorrelation, spatial plate patterns, and nonlinearity.
- Repeat analyses excluding one job, lot, printer, or test session at a time.
- Correct inferential claims for the prespecified family of primary factors or use hierarchical shrinkage.
- Keep confirmatory data untouched until the model and thresholds are locked.

## 7. Decision / stopping rule

Before data collection, define:

- the minimum practically important strength difference;
- the minimum useful variance reduction;
- acceptable uncertainty around each local sensitivity;
- required predictive accuracy;
- maximum feasible sample size and number of independent builds.

A factor will be judged explanatory only if:

1. Its randomized local perturbation produces a reproducible effect with the predicted direction.
2. The interval estimate excludes effects smaller than the practical threshold, or supports equivalence when testing a null explanation.
3. The effect appears in independent builds and is not attributable to measurement error, invalid failure, or uncontrolled drift.
4. Corresponding mechanistic observations are present.
5. Adding the factor or its validated mediator materially improves out-of-sample prediction or reduces the relevant variance component.
6. A targeted intervention based on the explanation changes strength as prospectively predicted.

Stop adding runs for a factor when sequentially evaluated, prespecified precision criteria are met: the sensitivity interval is narrow enough to classify the factor as practically important or practically negligible. Do not stop solely upon achieving nominal statistical significance.

Conclude that the measured factor set is adequate when confirmatory predictions meet the prespecified error and interval-coverage targets and residual between-condition variation is below the practical threshold. If unexplained variation remains, stop the current phase at the resource cap and classify the result as unresolved rather than selecting the best-fitting explanation without adequate discrimination.

## 8. Limitations

- Local sensitivities apply only near the selected baseline and may not extrapolate to other machines, polymers, geometries, orientations, or environmental ranges.
- Small perturbations may miss threshold behavior, while larger perturbations may leave the local regime.
- Some process factors are difficult to vary independently; commanded changes may alter several physical states simultaneously.
- Thermal, moisture, and extrusion measurements may be imperfect proxies for conditions at individual interfaces.
- Destructive tensile testing prevents within-coupon repeated outcomes and limits direct causal mediation analysis.
- Imaging or sectioning only a subset may introduce sampling uncertainty for internal structure.
- High-order interactions may remain unidentified because of practical limits on builds and specimens.
- Printer aging, firmware behavior, maintenance, and unrecorded control actions may create time-varying effects.
- Conditioning and test standards improve comparability but may not represent service conditions.
- Failure-mode changes can make a single “strength” response insufficient; conclusions must therefore be tied to fracture observations and secondary mechanical outcomes.
- Residual unexplained variation does not itself establish an unmeasured material mechanism; it may also reflect inadequate measurement resolution or model misspecification.

